## 7. A Quick Glossary of the Trickier Terms

- **Bag / Instance (Multiple Instance Learning):** Instead of labeling every
  single tiny tile of a slide, you only know the correct answer for the
  *whole slide*. So all the tiles from one slide are grouped into a "bag,"
  and the model has to figure out, on its own, which tiles (instances)
  inside that bag were the most important for reaching the final,
  bag-level answer.
- **Feature vector / embedding:** A list of numbers that summarizes what is
  visually important in an image tile, produced by a separate,
  already-trained encoder model — a compressed numerical "fingerprint" of
  the image rather than the raw pixels themselves.
- **Fold / Cross-validation:** A way of testing a model fairly by rotating
  which group of patients is used for testing versus training, so that
  every patient gets a turn as "unseen data," and the reported performance
  isn't just luck based on one particular split.
- **Checkpoint:** A saved snapshot of everything the model has learned so
  far, which can be reloaded later to keep training, to make new
  predictions, or to reproduce a result exactly.
- **Epoch:** One complete pass of the model looking at every training
  example exactly once.
- **Threshold:** Since the model actually outputs a probability (like "73%
  confident this is positive"), a threshold is the cutoff point above which
  a prediction counts as "positive" instead of "negative." The code
  automatically searches for the threshold value that gives the best
  trade-off between catching true cases and avoiding false alarms.
- **ROC curve / AUC:** A chart that shows how well a yes/no classifier
  performs across every possible threshold, plotting the trade-off between
  correctly caught positive cases and mistakenly flagged negative ones. AUC
  ("Area Under the Curve") condenses that whole chart into one number
  between 0.5 (no better than guessing) and 1.0 (perfect).
- **Precision, Recall, F1-score:** Precision asks "of everything the model
  called positive, how much was actually positive?" Recall asks "of
  everything that was actually positive, how much did the model correctly
  catch?" F1-score is a single number that balances both of these at once,
  which is especially useful when positive and negative cases aren't
  evenly balanced in the data (as is the case here).

